WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.015
Authorization failures occur when an application does not reliably verify whether the authenticated user is allowed to perform a specific action or access a specific payment record.

2
00:00:11.015 --> 00:00:16.513
Login alone is not enough; every sensitive request needs server-side authorization.

3
00:00:16.513 --> 00:00:23.956
Privilege escalation can give a lower-privileged user administrative capabilities or access to records belonging to another account.

4
00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:31.260
In payment environments this can expose transaction history, refunds, customer data, or administrative functions.

5
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:41.417
Access control bypasses often appear in APIs, hidden routes, disabled buttons, direct URL access, or client-controlled role values.

6
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:47.424
If the server trusts client-side decisions, attackers can often call the underlying function directly.

7
00:00:47.424 --> 00:01:00.355
Insecure direct object references happen when identifiers such as order IDs, customer IDs, invoice IDs, or payment record references are exposed without checking ownership or role-based permission.

8
00:01:00.355 --> 00:01:04.625
Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrators.

9
00:01:04.625 --> 00:01:08.327
Use role-based access control with explicit permission checks.

10
00:01:08.327 --> 00:01:12.758
Enforce authorization on the server for every sensitive object and action.

